1
00:00:16,266 --> 00:00:18,351
[ Thunder Rumbles ]

2
00:00:20,311 --> 00:00:22,605
[ Thunderclap ]

3
00:03:12,066 --> 00:03:15,195
[ Motor Whirring ]

4
00:03:15,278 --> 00:03:18,239
[ Motor Stops ]
[ Shouting ]
See how it's done?

5
00:03:18,323 --> 00:03:22,368
Yeah, I think I got
the hang of it.
You better.

6
00:03:22,410 --> 00:03:27,040
Boss'll chew my head off
if these cores get messed up.
Don't worry.

7
00:03:41,638 --> 00:03:43,806
We're at 26 feet.

8
00:03:43,890 --> 00:03:46,059
You let Jason
operate the drill?

9
00:03:46,142 --> 00:03:48,102
Yeah, he can
handle it.

10
00:04:11,542 --> 00:04:15,922
[ Motor Slows, Stops ]

11
00:04:15,964 --> 00:04:18,591
[ Crunching ]

12
00:04:18,633 --> 00:04:21,594
[ Cracking ]

13
00:04:38,736 --> 00:04:40,947
I didn't do
anything!

14
00:04:46,786 --> 00:04:49,747
Gimme your hand!
Let go of the drill!

15
00:04:57,547 --> 00:05:01,009
Forget it, Jack!
It's too late!

16
00:05:13,313 --> 00:05:15,315
You're not
gonna make it!

17
00:05:24,490 --> 00:05:26,826
Jack!

18
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:40,131
Jack!
Gimme your hand!

19
00:05:40,214 --> 00:05:43,176
[ Yelling ]

20
00:05:43,259 --> 00:05:45,178
I got you!

21
00:05:45,219 --> 00:05:48,765
[ All Shouting ]

22
00:05:48,848 --> 00:05:52,185
What were you thinking?
[ Sighs ]

23
00:05:52,226 --> 00:05:56,814
- What's happening?
- The whole damn shelf
is breaking off!

24
00:05:56,856 --> 00:05:59,359
That's
what's happening!

25
00:06:02,945 --> 00:06:05,698
[ Cracking, Rumbling Continue ]

26
00:06:17,001 --> 00:06:19,295
[ Jack ]
What we have found
locked in these ice cores...

27
00:06:19,378 --> 00:06:21,589
is evidence of a cataclysmic
climate shift,

28
00:06:21,672 --> 00:06:24,133
which occurred around
10,000 years ago.

29
00:06:24,217 --> 00:06:28,513
The concentration of these
natural greenhouse gases
in the ice cores...

30
00:06:28,596 --> 00:06:31,849
indicates that runaway warming
pushed the planet
into an ice age...

31
00:06:31,891 --> 00:06:34,227
which lasted two centuries.

32
00:06:34,268 --> 00:06:37,647
[ Speaking
Foreign Language ]
I'm confused.

33
00:06:37,730 --> 00:06:42,110
I thought you were talking
about global warming,
not an ice age.

34
00:06:42,193 --> 00:06:46,531
Yes, it is a paradox,
but global warming can trigger
a cooling trend.

35
00:06:46,572 --> 00:06:48,658
Let me explain.

36
00:06:48,741 --> 00:06:53,538
The northern hemisphere
owes its temperate climate
to the North Atlantic current.

37
00:06:53,579 --> 00:06:57,834
Heat from the sun arrives
at the equator and is
carried north by the ocean.

38
00:06:57,917 --> 00:07:03,714
But global warming
is melting the polar ice caps
and disrupting this flow.

39
00:07:03,756 --> 00:07:05,800
Eventually,
it will shut down.

40
00:07:05,883 --> 00:07:09,512
And when that occurs,
there goes
our warm climate.

41
00:07:09,595 --> 00:07:14,308
Excuse me.
When do you think this
could happen, Professor?

42
00:07:14,392 --> 00:07:16,227
When?
I don't know.

43
00:07:16,269 --> 00:07:18,229
Maybe in a hundred years.
Maybe in a thousand.

44
00:07:18,271 --> 00:07:21,149
But what I do know is
that if we do not act soon,

45
00:07:21,232 --> 00:07:25,111
it is our children
and our grandchildren
who will have to pay the price.

46
00:07:26,821 --> 00:07:29,782
And who's going to pay
the price of the Kyoto Accord?

47
00:07:29,866 --> 00:07:33,911
It would cost
the world's economy hundreds
of billions of dollars.

48
00:07:33,953 --> 00:07:36,414
With all due respect,
Mr. Vice President,

49
00:07:36,455 --> 00:07:39,500
the cost of doing nothing
could be even higher.

50
00:07:39,584 --> 00:07:42,170
Our climate is fragile.

51
00:07:42,253 --> 00:07:45,131
At the rate we're burning
fossil fuels and polluting
the environment,

52
00:07:45,173 --> 00:07:47,717
the ice caps
will soon disappear.

53
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:49,886
Professor, um, Hall,

54
00:07:51,345 --> 00:07:54,599
our economy is every bit
as fragile as the environment.

55
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:58,811
Perhaps you should keep that
in mind before making
sensationalist claims.

56
00:07:58,853 --> 00:08:02,398
Well, the last chunk of ice
that broke off...

57
00:08:02,482 --> 00:08:04,817
was about the size
of the state of Rhode Island.

58
00:08:04,901 --> 00:08:07,695
Some people might call that
pretty sensational.

59
00:08:07,778 --> 00:08:09,906
[ Men Chuckling ]

60
00:08:11,199 --> 00:08:13,326
[ People Shouting ]
Stop global warming!

61
00:08:13,409 --> 00:08:16,829
[ Man ]
I'm here at the Global Warming
Conference in New Delhi,

62
00:08:16,871 --> 00:08:19,123
where, if you can believe
your eyes, it's snowing.

63
00:08:19,165 --> 00:08:21,417
The coldest weather
on record...

64
00:08:21,501 --> 00:08:24,670
has thrown the city
into chaos with numbers
of homeless people--

65
00:08:24,754 --> 00:08:27,673
Taxi! Taxi!

66
00:08:27,757 --> 00:08:31,636
I enjoyed
your testimony, Professor.
It was very spirited.

67
00:08:31,677 --> 00:08:35,264
Oh, thank you.
It's what we're here for,
right? Put on a good show?

68
00:08:35,348 --> 00:08:38,726
Quite. Uh,
I was wondering if
I could talk to you...

69
00:08:38,809 --> 00:08:41,520
about your theory
on abrupt climate shift.

70
00:08:41,562 --> 00:08:43,689
The name's Rapson.
Terry Rapson.

71
00:08:43,773 --> 00:08:47,068
Professor Rapson?
Of the Hedland Center?
That's me.

72
00:08:47,151 --> 00:08:50,029
I've read your work
on ocean currents.

73
00:08:50,112 --> 00:08:52,490
What do you say
to a spot of tea?
Absolutely.

74
00:08:52,531 --> 00:08:54,617
If we can hail a cab.
Oh.

75
00:08:54,700 --> 00:08:57,620
[ Loud Whistle ]

76
00:08:57,662 --> 00:09:00,039
Over here!

77
00:09:00,122 --> 00:09:03,084
[ Thunder Rumbling ]
[ Bell Clanging ]

78
00:09:17,473 --> 00:09:19,600
[ Man ] Welcome back
to Glasgow, Scotland,
[ Beeping ]

79
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:21,310
We return
to the action 63 minutes
into the second half...

80
00:09:21,710 --> 00:09:25,210
[Edit: BBC football / Rapson snoring pad]

81
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:27,760
Scotland match VO + Rapson asleep; short establishment pad.

82
00:09:30,194 --> 00:09:34,031
Dennis, NOMAD buoy 4311
is showing a temperature drop...

83
00:09:34,073 --> 00:09:36,659
of 13 degrees.

84
00:09:36,701 --> 00:09:40,162
- Hmm? Where is 4311?
- Well, it's, uh,

85
00:09:43,833 --> 00:09:49,046
- Georges Bank.
- Oh, it's rough seas out there.
Must've knocked it about.

86
00:09:51,048 --> 00:09:54,719
[ Sheep Bleating ]
[ Gulls Squawking ]

87
00:09:57,722 --> 00:09:59,724
[ Television Chatter Continues ]

88
00:09:59,724 --> 00:10:00,474
[ Thunderclap ]
[ Announcement In Japanese ]

89
00:10:00,558 --> 00:10:02,560
[ Announcement Continues ]

90
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:06,460
[Edit: Rapson wake / India small talk]

91
00:10:06,610 --> 00:10:09,010
Lads winning + “How was India?” soft open after the snore beat.

92
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:14,864
- [ Sirens Chirping ]
- [ Speaking Japanese ]

93
00:10:16,490 --> 00:10:20,077
[ Cell Phone Ringing ]
[ Speaking Japanese ]

94
00:10:21,537 --> 00:10:25,041
[ Woman On Phone
Speaking Japanese ]
[ Speaking Japanese ]

95
00:10:27,710 --> 00:10:30,338
[ Woman Continues ]

96
00:10:45,019 --> 00:10:46,979
[ People Screaming ]

97
00:11:10,086 --> 00:11:15,466
[ Woman Continues ]

98
00:11:15,866 --> 00:11:19,366
[Edit: Sam–Lucy goodbye + Noelani + Rapson calls Jack]

99
00:11:22,014 --> 00:11:24,433
[ Man ]
Parker, this is Houston.

100
00:11:24,517 --> 00:11:27,436
We're seeing
some bad weather
over Canaveral.

101
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:29,605
Doesn't look like
you're coming back
this week.

102
00:11:29,689 --> 00:11:31,690
[ Chuckles ]
Your wife's gonna
give me an earful.

103
00:11:31,774 --> 00:11:34,360
[ Chuckles ]
Roger that.

104
00:11:34,402 --> 00:11:36,570
[ Man #2 ]
Hey, come take a look
at this storm system.

105
00:11:36,654 --> 00:11:38,531
It's enormous.

106
00:11:50,626 --> 00:11:53,129
[ Thunder Rumbling ]

107
00:11:55,631 --> 00:11:57,508
[ Thunderclap ]

108
00:11:57,908 --> 00:12:00,862
[Edit: Plane turbulence / “afraid of flying”]

109
00:12:01,262 --> 00:12:03,472
I can't believe
I'm spending two weeks
alone with my mum.

110
00:12:03,556 --> 00:12:06,809
Be patient with her, all right?
She's been looking forward
to this holiday for months.

111
00:12:06,892 --> 00:12:08,811
I know.
[ Both Chuckle ]

112
00:12:08,894 --> 00:12:13,232
I love you.
I love you too.

113
00:12:15,067 --> 00:12:19,447
Hey. Hey, hello.
Bye-bye.

114
00:12:19,488 --> 00:12:21,490
[ Man ] The cause
of this extreme weather
remains a mystery,

115
00:12:21,490 --> 00:12:25,995
although some meteorologists
believe sunspots are to blame.

116
00:12:26,036 --> 00:12:28,038
This is very odd.

117
00:12:28,122 --> 00:12:32,168
There's a buoy here
registering a 13 degree drop
in ocean temperature.

118
00:12:32,251 --> 00:12:34,253
Oh, yeah. That's right.

119
00:12:34,295 --> 00:12:36,672
That buoy malfunctioned
the other day.

120
00:12:36,756 --> 00:12:39,675
I'll put a call in,
see if there are any ships
near Georges Bank to get it.

121
00:12:39,717 --> 00:12:42,928
This buoy isn't
in Georges Bank.
It's just off Greenland.

122
00:12:43,012 --> 00:12:45,181
[ Man ]
What?

123
00:12:46,557 --> 00:12:48,893
What are the odds
of two buoys failing?

124
00:12:48,976 --> 00:12:50,811
Remote.

125
00:12:56,150 --> 00:12:59,195
Make that three.

126
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:10,780
[Edit: NYC traffic / Buddha exhaust lecture]

127
00:13:10,930 --> 00:13:13,359
J.D. traffic-jam pollution lecture on the way in — yuppie comic pad.

128
00:13:13,359 --> 00:13:16,729
[Edit: confirm on picture (no overlapping theatrical dialogue)]

129
00:13:17,129 --> 00:13:19,507
[ Barking ]

130
00:13:22,676 --> 00:13:25,054
- [ Roaring ]
- What's got into them?

131
00:13:25,137 --> 00:13:29,517
[ Animals Howling, Calling ]
I have no idea.
They're all worked up today.

132
00:13:29,558 --> 00:13:30,643
[ Howling ]

133
00:13:31,043 --> 00:13:34,543
[Edit: School quiz / Sam–Laura meetup]

134
00:13:35,397 --> 00:13:37,191
[ Phone Ringing ]

135
00:13:37,591 --> 00:13:39,991
Academic Decathlon quiz + Sam meeting Laura/Brian social beat.

136
00:13:40,141 --> 00:13:42,541
Academic Decathlon quiz + Sam–Laura meetup

137
00:13:43,113 --> 00:13:48,077
Who is it?
Terry Rapson here. Sorry
to call you so early.

138
00:13:48,160 --> 00:13:50,287
No, Professor.
That's all right.
What is it?

139
00:13:50,371 --> 00:13:53,749
Well, we've found
something extraordinary--

140
00:13:53,833 --> 00:13:56,377
extraordinary
and disturbing,
that is.

141
00:13:56,418 --> 00:13:58,546
You recall what you said
in New Delhi...

142
00:13:58,587 --> 00:14:02,258
about how
polar melting might disrupt
the North Atlantic current?

143
00:14:02,299 --> 00:14:04,134
Yes.

144
00:14:04,218 --> 00:14:06,011
Well,

145
00:14:06,095 --> 00:14:08,222
I think it's happening.

146
00:14:11,934 --> 00:14:14,645
What do you mean?
One of our
NOMAD buoys...

147
00:14:14,728 --> 00:14:18,732
registered a 13 degree drop
in surface temperature
the other day.

148
00:14:18,816 --> 00:14:21,110
I sent you
an e-mail.
Hold on.

149
00:14:23,529 --> 00:14:26,115
At first we thought
it was a malfunction,

150
00:14:26,156 --> 00:14:30,327
but there are four more
across the North Atlantic
showing the same thing.

151
00:14:30,411 --> 00:14:32,788
[ Jack ]
This is unbelievable.

152
00:14:32,830 --> 00:14:35,624
You predicted
it would happen.

153
00:14:35,708 --> 00:14:38,877
Yes, but not
in our lifetime.
This is too fast.

154
00:14:38,961 --> 00:14:44,341
There are no forecast models
remotely capable
of plotting this scenario,

155
00:14:44,425 --> 00:14:46,302
except yours.

156
00:14:46,385 --> 00:14:49,471
My model is a reconstruction
of a prehistoric climate shift.

157
00:14:49,555 --> 00:14:54,101
It's not a forecast model.
It's the closest thing
we have.

158
00:14:54,184 --> 00:14:57,980
Nothing like this
has every happened before.

159
00:14:58,022 --> 00:15:01,150
At least not in
the last 10,000 years.

160
00:15:27,176 --> 00:15:28,344
[ Ringing ]

161
00:15:28,394 --> 00:15:29,894
[Edit: Noelani swell TV weatherman]

162
00:15:29,894 --> 00:15:31,180
Shouldn't you be monitoring
the weather or something?

163
00:15:31,263 --> 00:15:33,766
This is L.A.
What weather?

164
00:15:36,060 --> 00:15:38,979
[ Motor Running, Faint ]
Wait.
What's that noise?

165
00:15:39,021 --> 00:15:41,857
What noise?

166
00:15:41,940 --> 00:15:46,070
[ Motor Running, Louder ]

167
00:15:46,153 --> 00:15:48,447
[ Motor Stops ]
[ Phone Ringing ]

168
00:15:50,032 --> 00:15:54,203
[ Sighs ]
Honey, I'll be
right back.

169
00:15:56,038 --> 00:15:57,081
[ Woman ]
The coast guard has
closed the beaches...

170
00:15:58,040 --> 00:16:00,376
as waves have been too big
for even the most experienced
of surfers.

171
00:16:00,417 --> 00:16:02,670
L.A. Weather Center.
It's Tommy!
I'm down at the beach!

172
00:16:02,711 --> 00:16:04,713
I'm in the middle
of something.
What do you need?

173
00:16:04,797 --> 00:16:07,716
There-- Ooh! Uh.

174
00:16:07,758 --> 00:16:10,761
There's-- There's hail
the size of golf balls
coming down here!

175
00:16:10,844 --> 00:16:12,846
[ Tommy Continues, Indistinct ]
[ Man ] Low pressure system
moving along...

176
00:16:12,888 --> 00:16:16,266
the California coastline
is creating a cyclonic system
across the L.A. basin.

177
00:16:16,350 --> 00:16:19,478
[ Phone Ringing ]

178
00:16:23,524 --> 00:16:26,110
Yeah. Hello.
Hey, boss, turn on
the Weather Channel.

179
00:16:26,193 --> 00:16:28,112
I think we have to issue
a tornado warning.

180
00:16:28,195 --> 00:16:30,823
What are
you talking about?

181
00:16:30,906 --> 00:16:32,866
[ Woman ]
Palmdale and Lancaster
are reporting...

182
00:16:32,908 --> 00:16:35,369
wind speeds in excess
of 70 miles per hour,
Hold on a second.

183
00:16:35,452 --> 00:16:37,621
conditions that are
highly unusual for California.

184
00:16:56,771 --> 00:16:59,869
[Edit: Tom / mainframe access ask]

185
00:17:00,269 --> 00:17:03,647
[ Woman ]
We have live coverage now
from our Fox 11 chopper.

186
00:17:03,689 --> 00:17:06,275
Are you there, Bart?
Uh, yes, I'm here.

187
00:17:06,358 --> 00:17:09,445
These tornadoes are forming
so fast-- What?

188
00:17:09,528 --> 00:17:12,698
[ Bart ]
Oh! Oh, my God!
Lisa--

189
00:17:12,740 --> 00:17:16,410
Are you getting this on camera?
This tornado just came
and erased the Hollywood sign.

190
00:17:16,493 --> 00:17:20,122
The Hollywood sign is gone.
It's just shredded.

191
00:17:20,205 --> 00:17:23,667
[ Wind Roaring ]
[ People Screaming ]

192
00:17:32,092 --> 00:17:34,386
[ Lisa ]
Bart, what can you see?
Is anyone hurt?

193
00:17:34,470 --> 00:17:37,181
[ Bart ]
I wouldn't be surprised. There
is so much damage down there.

194
00:17:37,222 --> 00:17:40,059
And there are people
down there taking pictures!

195
00:17:40,142 --> 00:17:42,352
Hey! What the hell
are you guys doing!

196
00:17:42,394 --> 00:17:45,981
Go for cover!
You can't stay here!
Get outta here!

197
00:17:48,609 --> 00:17:53,697
What you're seeing are two
actual tornadoes striking Los
Angeles International Airport.

198
00:17:53,739 --> 00:17:56,909
Wait. Wait. It looks like
they've joined and formed
one large tornado.

199
00:17:56,992 --> 00:18:00,370
Tommy!
Oh, my God!

200
00:18:02,414 --> 00:18:05,292
[ Man ]
Urge all of our viewers to stay
away from the area.

201
00:18:05,375 --> 00:18:10,255
- Jeff, where are you?
- I'm on, uh, Yucca and Vine.
I'm on my way in now.

202
00:18:10,339 --> 00:18:12,257
Jeff, you're on TV!
You're right
in the middle of it!

203
00:18:12,341 --> 00:18:14,426
God. Oh, my God!

204
00:18:21,934 --> 00:18:24,102
You gotta get
out of there, man!

205
00:18:24,186 --> 00:18:26,271
Oh.

206
00:18:28,774 --> 00:18:30,651
[ Bart ]
That bus just got dropped
on top of that Porsche!

207
00:18:30,734 --> 00:18:34,571
[ Line Busy Tone ]
Oh, my God. I hope
no one was in that car!

208
00:18:34,613 --> 00:18:36,615
[ Woman ]
And for our national audience
just joining us now,

209
00:18:36,657 --> 00:18:38,784
we are going live
to downtown Los Angeles
right now.

210
00:18:38,867 --> 00:18:42,079
- Tommy?
- If you look over there
behind me, that's a tornado.

211
00:18:43,080 --> 00:18:45,624
Yes! A twister
in Los Angeles.

212
00:18:45,707 --> 00:18:50,045
It's one of the many
tornadoes that are
destroying our city.

213
00:18:50,129 --> 00:18:53,590
There's another one!
That's the Los Angeles
skyline!

214
00:18:53,632 --> 00:18:57,177
It's unbelievable!
It's huge! I've never
seen anything like that!

215
00:18:58,428 --> 00:19:00,973
[ Gasping ]

216
00:19:08,814 --> 00:19:10,858
What's happening?

217
00:19:11,258 --> 00:19:14,758
[Edit: Tornado TV “nightmare” sound bite]

218
00:19:14,908 --> 00:19:17,308
Short news horror one-liner over LA coverage.

219
00:19:27,916 --> 00:19:31,295
[ Rumbling Stops ]

220
00:19:45,017 --> 00:19:48,228
[ Wind Howling ]

221
00:19:55,402 --> 00:19:58,322
[ Car Alarms Blaring ]

222
00:20:17,591 --> 00:20:19,843
[ Chattering ]

223
00:20:19,893 --> 00:20:21,211
[Edit: Oval Office tornado briefing]

224
00:20:21,261 --> 00:20:23,555
All right. All right.
Listen up, everybody.

225
00:20:23,639 --> 00:20:27,768
Listen up, please.
We've got a lot of work to do
and we don't have much time.

226
00:20:27,809 --> 00:20:31,939
So, let's get started,
please. Vorsteen?

227
00:20:32,022 --> 00:20:34,608
All our grid models
are worthless.

228
00:20:34,650 --> 00:20:36,818
I don't think grid models
are gonna be a lot of help here.

229
00:20:36,902 --> 00:20:40,614
The Canadians are reporting
tremendous circulation
moving down from the Arctic.

230
00:20:40,697 --> 00:20:44,034
In Siberia, there's
a low pressure system
unlike anything we've seen,

231
00:20:44,076 --> 00:20:46,912
and Australia just saw
the strongest typhoon
ever recorded.

232
00:20:46,995 --> 00:20:49,081
[ Man ]
Hang on. Are you saying that
these things are interconnected?

233
00:20:50,082 --> 00:20:51,875
[ Booker ]
We have to consider
the possibility.

234
00:20:51,917 --> 00:20:54,878
The only force strong enough
to affect global weather
is the sun.

235
00:20:54,920 --> 00:20:59,758
- What's NASA have to say?
- We've already checked.
Solar output is normal.

236
00:20:59,841 --> 00:21:04,888
What about
the North Atlantic current?
[ Vorsteen ] What about it?

237
00:21:04,972 --> 00:21:07,975
I got a call last night
from Professor Rapson
at the Hedland Center.

238
00:21:08,934 --> 00:21:10,769
He thinks the current
has changed.

239
00:21:10,852 --> 00:21:12,896
[ Murmuring ]

240
00:21:12,980 --> 00:21:15,274
Come on, Jack.
How could that be?

241
00:21:15,357 --> 00:21:18,193
The current depends upon
a delicate balance of salt
and fresh water.

242
00:21:18,235 --> 00:21:21,655
- We all know that.
- Yes.

243
00:21:21,738 --> 00:21:23,740
But no one has
taken into account...

244
00:21:23,824 --> 00:21:28,203
how much fresh water
has been dumped into the ocean
because of melting polar ice.

245
00:21:28,245 --> 00:21:30,914
I think we've hit
a critical desalinization point.

246
00:21:30,998 --> 00:21:33,166
[ Murmuring ]

247
00:21:34,793 --> 00:21:37,045
It would explain
what's driving
this extreme weather.

248
00:21:37,087 --> 00:21:40,007
Hedland had some pretty
convincing data.

249
00:21:40,048 --> 00:21:44,011
They've asked me to feed it into
my paleoclimate model to track
the next set of events.

250
00:21:44,094 --> 00:21:47,889
Hold on, Jack. Are you
suggesting these weather
anomalies are gonna continue?

251
00:21:47,931 --> 00:21:50,976
Not just continue--
get worse.

252
00:21:55,105 --> 00:21:57,607
I think we're on the verge
of a major climate shift.

253
00:21:57,691 --> 00:22:00,193
[ Murmuring ]

254
00:22:02,612 --> 00:22:05,782
Tom!
What are you gonna tell
the administration?

255
00:22:05,866 --> 00:22:07,701
What do you expect me
to tell them?

256
00:22:07,784 --> 00:22:10,579
The government has
to start making long-term
preparations now.

257
00:22:10,662 --> 00:22:13,540
Jack, all you have is a theory.
Then give me the mainframe
and let me prove it!

258
00:22:13,623 --> 00:22:16,626
No.

259
00:22:17,794 --> 00:22:19,796
You have 48 hours.

260
00:22:19,838 --> 00:22:21,840
[ Sighs ]
Professor Hall.

261
00:22:21,923 --> 00:22:24,301
Yes?
I think your theory
may be correct.

262
00:22:24,384 --> 00:22:25,969
Walk with me.

263
00:22:26,011 --> 00:22:29,890
Just a few weeks ago,
I monitored the strongest
hurricane on record.

264
00:22:29,973 --> 00:22:32,601
The hail, the tornadoes--
it all fits.

265
00:22:32,642 --> 00:22:35,228
Can the model
you're working on
factor in storm scenarios?

266
00:22:35,312 --> 00:22:38,815
Oh, we haven't had
the time.
Maybe I can help.

267
00:22:38,899 --> 00:22:40,484
Welcome aboard.
Thanks.

268
00:22:40,884 --> 00:22:44,384
[Edit: Lucy hospital / Peter CT]

269
00:22:46,656 --> 00:22:50,035
Jack. Jack.
We got the results.

270
00:22:50,435 --> 00:22:52,835
Lucy at the hospital with Peter’s scan — domestic subplot pad.

271
00:22:52,985 --> 00:22:56,021
Lucy hospital / Peter CT, Miami photo nostalgia, “wish I could have seen him grow up”

272
00:22:58,794 --> 00:23:01,088
Six to eight months.
That can't be.

273
00:23:01,129 --> 00:23:04,883
That time scale
isn't in months.
It's in weeks.

274
00:23:08,553 --> 00:23:10,389
So far, the terrible weather
hasn't hit the D.C. area,

275
00:23:10,439 --> 00:23:11,939
[Edit: Flood news + Sam housing call]

276
00:23:11,939 --> 00:23:13,183
but local residents
aren't taking any chances,

277
00:23:13,225 --> 00:23:15,852
as people stock up
for what is already
being billed...

278
00:23:15,894 --> 00:23:18,563
as the worst storm season
on record.

279
00:23:18,647 --> 00:23:21,066
You better be sure
about this, Jack.
My ass is on the line.

280
00:23:21,149 --> 00:23:24,236
You saw the model.
And I hope to God
it's wrong.

281
00:23:24,319 --> 00:23:26,696
Oh.
Mr. Vice President.
Tom.

282
00:23:26,738 --> 00:23:29,449
You know
Professor Hall.
Yes, we've met.

283
00:23:29,533 --> 00:23:33,036
Uh, Professor Hall has some
new information I think
you should take a look at.

284
00:23:33,078 --> 00:23:35,872
We just got these results
from our simulation model.

285
00:23:35,956 --> 00:23:38,041
They explain what's causing
the severe weather.

286
00:23:38,125 --> 00:23:41,711
I'll look at this later.
I have a meeting with
the director of FEMA now.

287
00:23:41,753 --> 00:23:44,548
This is very urgent, sir.
Our climate
is changing violently.

288
00:23:44,631 --> 00:23:47,217
It's going to happen
over the next six
to eight weeks.

289
00:23:47,300 --> 00:23:50,053
I thought you said this
wouldn't happen for another
hundred years or so.

290
00:23:50,137 --> 00:23:52,722
I was wrong.
Well, suppose
you're wrong this time.

291
00:23:52,764 --> 00:23:56,726
I wish that I were, but I'm sure
that you're aware of what's
happening all around the world.

292
00:23:56,768 --> 00:24:00,272
We're making all the necessary
preparations for this storm.
What more do you expect?

293
00:24:00,355 --> 00:24:04,276
You have to start
thinking about large scale
evacuations right now,

294
00:24:04,359 --> 00:24:06,319
especially in
the northern states.

295
00:24:06,403 --> 00:24:08,447
Evacuations?
Yes.

296
00:24:08,530 --> 00:24:12,367
Have you lost
your mind, Hall?
I have to go.

297
00:24:12,409 --> 00:24:17,372
Mr. Vice President,
if we don't act now,
it's going to be too late.

298
00:24:21,251 --> 00:24:23,086
Come on, Jack.

299
00:24:23,564 --> 00:24:25,064
[Edit: Natural History Museum stuffed-animals gag]

300
00:24:26,006 --> 00:24:29,342
[ Man ]
It's been 24 hours now since
the snow started falling...

301
00:24:29,384 --> 00:24:31,303
across the British Isles
and over northern Europe,

302
00:24:31,386 --> 00:24:33,513
and it shows no signs
of letting up.

303
00:24:33,555 --> 00:24:36,183
No. No, no, no.
You gotta stop worrying.
No.

304
00:24:36,224 --> 00:24:40,270
It's fine. We got plenty
of supplies with us.
We're just snowed in.

305
00:24:40,353 --> 00:24:43,356
Yeah. No, it's all right.
It's all right.

306
00:24:43,398 --> 00:24:45,358
You stay
where you are, okay?
I'll be fine.

307
00:24:45,400 --> 00:24:49,237
Yeah, I love you too.
Okay. Bye.
[ Sighs ]

308
00:24:49,279 --> 00:24:53,825
- Hey. How's Jeanette?
- Oh, fine.

309
00:24:53,909 --> 00:24:58,914
Ferry just landed.
Must be nice in Spain.
I wish I was there.

310
00:24:58,997 --> 00:25:01,583
[ TV: Reporter ]
The search and rescue team has
been deployed by helicopter...

311
00:25:01,666 --> 00:25:04,544
to airlift
the royal family to safety.

312
00:25:04,586 --> 00:25:06,713
You think
they'll come get us,
since we got snowed in?

313
00:25:06,755 --> 00:25:09,174
Not likely.

314
00:25:09,257 --> 00:25:11,218
Luckily,
we got our own genny,

315
00:25:11,259 --> 00:25:13,762
enough tea and biscuits
to sink a ship.

316
00:25:13,803 --> 00:25:17,349
Oh, we'll be fine.
As long as the loo
doesn't back up again.

317
00:25:17,432 --> 00:25:19,434
[ All Chuckle ]

318
00:25:22,270 --> 00:25:26,566
In Ireland, those remaining
in Belfast and Banbridge
have been urged to evacuate...

319
00:25:26,608 --> 00:25:29,778
to Dublin as soon
as possible.

320
00:25:29,861 --> 00:25:33,949
The north continues
to receive the worst
of the storm--

321
00:25:33,990 --> 00:25:35,951
[ Helicopter Approaching ]

322
00:25:38,495 --> 00:25:41,790
[ Man ]
We've got zero visibility.
Maintain heading and speed.

323
00:25:41,831 --> 00:25:44,167
- [ Man #2 ]
What's our heading?
- Zero, three, five, sir.

324
00:25:44,251 --> 00:25:47,462
Approximately 40 kilometers
from Balmoral Castle.

325
00:25:47,504 --> 00:25:49,631
[ Man ]
We've lost visual contact
with you.

326
00:26:13,655 --> 00:26:15,657
This gauge here
can't be right.

327
00:26:15,699 --> 00:26:17,659
[ Man #3 ]
Wind speed has dropped to zero.

328
00:26:17,742 --> 00:26:20,245
We have vertical walls
of cirrus cloud formations.

329
00:26:20,328 --> 00:26:21,830
[ Man ]
What the hell
is going on?

330
00:26:21,871 --> 00:26:23,832
Bloody fuel lines
are starting to freeze!

331
00:26:28,336 --> 00:26:30,839
[ Men Shouting ]

332
00:26:35,051 --> 00:26:38,972
Port engine pressure's dropping!
The rotor R.P.M. is dropping!

333
00:26:39,055 --> 00:26:41,099
We've got a flame-out
on the starboard side as well!

334
00:26:45,687 --> 00:26:47,856
Prepare
for crash landing!

335
00:26:49,357 --> 00:26:52,360
Select emergency fuel!

336
00:26:52,402 --> 00:26:55,030
Come on,
you bastard!

337
00:26:55,113 --> 00:26:57,490
Come on!

338
00:27:10,795 --> 00:27:13,173
[ Breathing Heavily ]

339
00:27:20,263 --> 00:27:23,183
[ Rapson ]
What I'm about to tell you
is supposed to be confidential.

340
00:27:23,224 --> 00:27:27,062
Several hours ago,
three helicopters went down
over Scotland.

341
00:27:27,145 --> 00:27:30,649
They crashed because the fuel
in their lines froze.

342
00:27:30,732 --> 00:27:32,609
At what temperature does--

343
00:27:32,692 --> 00:27:37,864
[ Rapson On Speaker ]
Negative 150 degrees Fahrenheit.
We had to look it up.

344
00:27:37,906 --> 00:27:40,158
The temperature dropped
phenomenally fast.

345
00:27:40,241 --> 00:27:43,578
On the ground, people froze
before they could
get out of their cars.

346
00:27:43,620 --> 00:27:47,707
- Get me a satellite picture
of Scotland two hours ago.
- Yeah.

347
00:27:47,749 --> 00:27:52,170
We've got mountains of data
but nowhere near enough
computer power to analyze it.

348
00:27:52,253 --> 00:27:55,590
- Can you help us?
- Send us what you've got,
and we'll do our best.

349
00:27:55,674 --> 00:27:59,260
[ Rapson ]
Thanks, Jack. Bye for now.

350
00:27:59,302 --> 00:28:02,639
This is Scotland at the time
the temperature dropped.

351
00:28:05,100 --> 00:28:07,894
[ Frank ]
This thing looks
just like a hurricane.

352
00:28:07,936 --> 00:28:10,772
[ Janet ]
Only hurricanes don't form
over land.

353
00:28:11,981 --> 00:28:14,359
It's a mob scene here
at Grand Central Station.

354
00:28:14,409 --> 00:28:15,909
[Edit: Jack home arrival / Laura “you live here?”]

355
00:28:15,909 --> 00:28:16,653
Over half the platforms
are flooded,

356
00:28:16,736 --> 00:28:19,572
and service has been
suspended on all trains.

357
00:28:19,656 --> 00:28:24,160
With planes still grounded
and trains now out of service,
that's bad news--

358
00:28:24,244 --> 00:28:27,872
Victor's coming to pick me up.
Do you guys want a ride
to the train station?

359
00:28:27,956 --> 00:28:31,126
Stranded
since this morning.
Not anymore.

360
00:28:31,167 --> 00:28:35,588
In Nova Scotia earlier,
the ocean rose by 25 feet
in a matter of seconds.

361
00:28:35,672 --> 00:28:39,968
What we have feared
for the past few days
has indeed happened.

362
00:28:40,009 --> 00:28:42,178
The cold front moving down
from the Arctic...

363
00:28:42,262 --> 00:28:45,098
has created an enormous
storm system in Canada,

364
00:28:45,181 --> 00:28:48,518
which, incredible as it sounds,
looks like a tropical hurricane.

365
00:28:48,601 --> 00:28:52,105
I gotta go pick up
my little brother.
Do you guys want a ride?

366
00:28:52,188 --> 00:28:55,483
Where is he?
He's in a boarding school
in Philadelphia.

367
00:28:55,525 --> 00:29:00,363
If this system moves south,
we could see a wind-driven
storm surge...

368
00:29:00,447 --> 00:29:03,700
that could threaten
the entire Eastern Seaboard.

369
00:29:03,783 --> 00:29:07,871
Okay. Bye. Victor's stuck
in traffic on Fifth Avenue.

370
00:29:07,954 --> 00:29:10,081
It'll be easier
to head out of town
if we meet him over there.

371
00:29:11,040 --> 00:29:14,127
All right.
You mean walk?
No, not in this.

372
00:29:15,420 --> 00:29:17,922
[ Thunder Rumbling ]

373
00:29:18,006 --> 00:29:20,383
We should take
the stairs.

374
00:29:20,467 --> 00:29:23,094
We're on the top floor.

375
00:29:25,346 --> 00:29:27,182
I guess we're walkin'.

376
00:29:29,642 --> 00:29:31,853
[ Thunderclap ]

377
00:29:31,936 --> 00:29:35,231
[ Horns Honking,
People Shouting ]

378
00:29:39,569 --> 00:29:43,907
Maybe we should just stay here.
I think the young lady
is right, sir.

379
00:29:43,990 --> 00:29:46,910
No. We need to get home.

380
00:29:46,951 --> 00:29:50,330
Come on!
[ Shouting Continues ]

381
00:29:59,631 --> 00:30:02,842
[ Goats Bleating ]

382
00:30:05,386 --> 00:30:07,680
[ Growls ]

383
00:30:11,601 --> 00:30:13,770
Hey, Cesar, come here.

384
00:30:13,812 --> 00:30:15,897
What are you doing?

385
00:30:20,652 --> 00:30:23,530
The wolves!
They're gone!

386
00:30:23,613 --> 00:30:26,241
[ Man On News ]
Just to give you an idea
of the situation,

387
00:30:26,282 --> 00:30:29,410
which seems to be becoming
worse with each passing minute,

388
00:30:29,452 --> 00:30:33,456
at the moment, we have flooding
in most parts of the island.

389
00:30:33,540 --> 00:30:36,501
We've got traffic snarl-ups
because the electricity
is now out...

390
00:30:36,584 --> 00:30:39,462
to almost every part
of Manhattan.

391
00:30:39,546 --> 00:30:43,049
No traffic signals,
car accidents-- at least 200--

392
00:30:43,132 --> 00:30:47,262
and Lower Manhattan, we've been
told, is virtually inaccessible.

393
00:30:47,345 --> 00:30:51,891
[ Man ]
No one is to be on the streets.
Please go inside.

394
00:30:51,933 --> 00:30:55,353
[ Excited Chattering ]
Sir, just calm down.

395
00:30:57,188 --> 00:30:58,606
Public library's right up here.
Make your way up there.
You can stay warm and dry.

396
00:30:59,206 --> 00:31:00,706
[Edit: Library dog / phone ask]

397
00:31:00,706 --> 00:31:02,652
Come on, guys.
This way.

398
00:31:02,735 --> 00:31:05,071
Excuse me.
Your bad.

399
00:31:05,154 --> 00:31:08,116
Goddamn $1,500
waterproof raincoat!

400
00:31:08,157 --> 00:31:10,034
Please shut up, man.

401
00:31:10,118 --> 00:31:12,954
There must be rats
everywhere.
That's 'cause it's New York.

402
00:31:12,996 --> 00:31:17,041
Hey! Hey!
Open up, big poppa!

403
00:31:17,125 --> 00:31:20,795
It's out of service.
It's out of service!

404
00:31:20,879 --> 00:31:23,756
No, no. I'll give you
$100 to put it in service.
You don't have to do that.

405
00:31:23,840 --> 00:31:27,260
No, no. Really, $200.
I won't have it.
I won't have it.

406
00:31:27,343 --> 00:31:30,138
Oh, God, I love buses.
This is just so much fun.

407
00:31:30,179 --> 00:31:32,682
[ Shouting ]
Hello?

408
00:31:32,765 --> 00:31:35,393
I can't reach my driver.
I lost the signal.

409
00:31:35,476 --> 00:31:38,146
This is insane.
We should just go back
to your apartment.

410
00:31:38,187 --> 00:31:40,148
[ Brian ]
Yeah. I vote for that.

411
00:31:40,189 --> 00:31:42,817
What? Are you kidding?
We have to get higher!

412
00:31:42,859 --> 00:31:45,695
Come on.
Up to the library.

413
00:31:45,695 --> 00:31:48,948
[ Thunderclap ]

414
00:31:49,348 --> 00:31:52,848
[Edit: French taxi / “I don’t speak French”]

415
00:31:52,998 --> 00:31:55,398
Laura’s French-speaker rescue gag in the flooded street.

416
00:31:55,548 --> 00:31:58,405
French-taxi / passport gags, body-heat warming lectures, “tell her how you feel”

417
00:32:10,470 --> 00:32:13,222
[ Shouting Continues ]

418
00:32:14,557 --> 00:32:16,851
- [ Child Screams ]
- [ Thunderclap ]

419
00:32:17,251 --> 00:32:20,751
[Edit: Laura eyes / cover-their-eyes beat]

420
00:32:20,901 --> 00:32:23,301
Laura managing the French family during the surge.

421
00:32:34,619 --> 00:32:37,914
[ Man On News ]
There is a wall of water
coming towards New York City!

422
00:32:37,956 --> 00:32:39,832
Everybody--

423
00:32:41,167 --> 00:32:43,628
[ Shouting, Screaming ]

424
00:32:47,465 --> 00:32:50,385
- [ Woman Screams ]
- [ Shouting Continues ]

425
00:33:19,185 --> 00:33:22,685
[Edit: Passports left in the cab]

426
00:33:22,835 --> 00:33:25,235
Bag → Laura goes back for passports.

427
00:33:25,837 --> 00:33:27,839
Oh, my--

428
00:33:33,928 --> 00:33:36,014
Laura!

429
00:33:40,935 --> 00:33:43,354
Laura!

430
00:33:43,438 --> 00:33:45,273
No! No!
No, Sam! Sam, no!

431
00:33:45,356 --> 00:33:49,569
Laura! Laura! Laura!
Come on!
[ Gasps ]

432
00:33:49,610 --> 00:33:52,864
Come! Come on!

433
00:33:54,198 --> 00:33:56,993
- Sam!
- Go!

434
00:34:15,261 --> 00:34:18,639
[ Dog Barking ]

435
00:34:32,824 --> 00:34:34,324
[Edit: Rapson grandson drawing small talk]

436
00:34:34,324 --> 00:34:35,531
Here he is.

437
00:34:37,742 --> 00:34:41,579
Jack, were you able
to re-create the thermal cycle?

438
00:34:41,662 --> 00:34:46,876
Yes. The storm's rotation
is pulling super-cooled air
down from the upper troposphere.

439
00:34:50,004 --> 00:34:54,008
But shouldn't the air warm up
before it reaches ground level?

440
00:34:54,050 --> 00:34:58,554
It should, but it doesn't.
The air's descending
too rapidly.

441
00:34:58,638 --> 00:35:01,808
Is this
an isolated incident?

442
00:35:04,227 --> 00:35:06,229
I'm afraid not.

443
00:35:06,270 --> 00:35:10,024
We've located two other
supercells in addition
to the one over Scotland.

444
00:35:10,066 --> 00:35:14,362
There's one over Northern Canada
and another one over Siberia.

445
00:35:14,403 --> 00:35:17,949
And do we know
their projected path?

446
00:35:18,032 --> 00:35:22,578
Yes. Our previous estimates
of six to eight weeks
weren't even close.

447
00:35:22,620 --> 00:35:26,249
This one storm is going
to change the face
of our planet.

448
00:35:26,332 --> 00:35:29,460
Here's a projection
of 24 hours out.

449
00:35:34,423 --> 00:35:37,343
This is 48 hours out.

450
00:35:39,303 --> 00:35:41,597
And in seven to 10 days.

451
00:35:45,143 --> 00:35:49,438
When this storm is over,
we'll be in a new ice age.

452
00:35:54,152 --> 00:35:56,070
My God.

453
00:36:14,463 --> 00:36:18,509
Professor, it's time
you got out of there.

454
00:36:20,219 --> 00:36:23,639
I'm afraid that time
has come and gone, my friend.

455
00:36:28,477 --> 00:36:30,938
What can we do?

456
00:36:34,066 --> 00:36:36,652
Save as many as you can.

457
00:36:36,694 --> 00:36:39,697
[ Feedback On Line ]

458
00:36:41,699 --> 00:36:44,118
[ Disconnect Signal ]

459
00:36:47,455 --> 00:36:50,416
[ Phone Ringing ]

460
00:36:51,667 --> 00:36:55,755
Jack. Something's
happened in New York.

461
00:37:05,848 --> 00:37:09,060
No. The power is out.
I've been in here all day.

462
00:37:09,143 --> 00:37:12,355
[ Officer ]
Who needs help?
Over here. Over here.

463
00:37:12,396 --> 00:37:14,857
[ Indistinct Shouting ]
Hi.

464
00:37:14,899 --> 00:37:17,652
[ Man ]
Hello.
[ Dog Whimpering ]

465
00:37:18,945 --> 00:37:21,530
What?

466
00:37:21,614 --> 00:37:26,661
This is the last one.
Enjoy it. Greedy.

467
00:37:26,702 --> 00:37:29,247
[ Operator ]
All circuits are busy.

468
00:37:29,247 --> 00:37:31,040
[ Dialing Phone ]

469
00:37:31,090 --> 00:37:32,590
[Edit: Library body-heat / “tell her how you feel”]

470
00:37:33,834 --> 00:37:38,547
No. There's still no service.
Damn cell phones.

471
00:37:38,947 --> 00:37:41,347
Sam/Laura warming + J.D. romance nudge after the surge.

472
00:37:41,926 --> 00:37:44,011
- Excuse me.
- Yes?

473
00:37:44,053 --> 00:37:46,847
Are there any pay phones
on the upper floors?
No. No.

474
00:37:46,889 --> 00:37:49,684
But there are some
on the mezzanine.
Great.

475
00:37:49,725 --> 00:37:52,144
- But I think it's underwater.
- Where are you going?

476
00:37:52,186 --> 00:37:56,107
The power's out.
Older pay phones draw their
power directly from the line.

477
00:37:56,190 --> 00:37:58,734
[ Woman On P.A., Indistinct ]

478
00:37:58,818 --> 00:38:02,530
Oh, God. I've been trying
to reach Sam.

479
00:38:02,571 --> 00:38:06,784
So have I.
I kept trying to call you,
but I couldn't get through.

480
00:38:06,867 --> 00:38:09,245
It's been a madhouse
around here. Come on.

481
00:38:14,417 --> 00:38:16,794
Are you sure about this?

482
00:38:19,714 --> 00:38:22,258
Ah. Oh.

483
00:38:24,593 --> 00:38:28,222
- [ Dial Tone ]
- It works.

484
00:38:28,222 --> 00:38:32,768
Jack? Sam's on the phone.
Line four.

485
00:38:32,818 --> 00:38:34,318
[Edit: Jack–Lucy Miami photo nostalgia]

486
00:38:35,438 --> 00:38:38,441
Where are you?
Are you all right?
We're at the public library.

487
00:38:38,524 --> 00:38:42,445
Sam, it's Mom.
I'm so happy you're okay.

488
00:38:42,528 --> 00:38:47,241
Mom, can you call
Laura and Brian's parents
and tell them we're all right?

489
00:38:47,283 --> 00:38:49,535
Yes. Of course.

490
00:38:49,618 --> 00:38:53,330
- Sam, what's that noise?
- Sam? Sam?

491
00:38:53,414 --> 00:38:58,461
[ Gasping ]
What is going on out there, Dad?

492
00:38:58,544 --> 00:39:02,631
Sam? Sam, listen to me.
Listen very carefully.

493
00:39:02,715 --> 00:39:05,343
Forget what I said
about trying to head south.

494
00:39:05,426 --> 00:39:08,429
It's too late for that.
The storm is just gonna
get worse.

495
00:39:08,471 --> 00:39:12,600
It's gonna turn into
a massive blizzard with an eye
in the center, like a hurricane.

496
00:39:12,641 --> 00:39:15,352
- Uh-huh.
- The air is gonna be so cold,

497
00:39:15,436 --> 00:39:18,064
you could freeze to death
in seconds.

498
00:39:20,357 --> 00:39:23,652
- Sam?
- What should we do?

499
00:39:23,736 --> 00:39:26,947
Listen to me, son.
Do not go outside.

500
00:39:26,989 --> 00:39:30,743
Just burn whatever you can
to stay warm and try
to wait it out.

501
00:39:30,785 --> 00:39:34,163
I will come for you.
Do you understand me?
I will come for you.

502
00:39:34,205 --> 00:39:36,624
[ Shivering ]

503
00:39:36,707 --> 00:39:40,669
Sam! Sam, come back!

504
00:39:40,711 --> 00:39:43,881
[ Jack ]
Sam? Sam, did you hear me?

505
00:39:43,964 --> 00:39:46,008
Did you hear me?

506
00:39:47,839 --> 00:39:51,339
[Edit: Lucy sobs Sam will be okay]

507
00:39:51,489 --> 00:39:53,889
Lucy’s panic call beat after the flood news.

508
00:40:05,444 --> 00:40:08,739
[ Gasping ]

509
00:40:08,781 --> 00:40:11,242
[ Shivering, Panting ]

510
00:40:11,283 --> 00:40:14,120
[ Sobbing ]
I thought you drowned.

511
00:40:19,125 --> 00:40:23,212
Let's find some
dry clothes for you.
Yeah.

512
00:40:28,759 --> 00:40:31,637
Where did you store
the arctic gear?

513
00:40:31,720 --> 00:40:34,056
You can't make it
to New York, Jack.

514
00:40:34,140 --> 00:40:36,976
I've walked that far
before in the snow.

515
00:40:37,059 --> 00:40:41,605
This is not the same.
Jack, this is not the same!

516
00:40:41,647 --> 00:40:43,732
Lucy, tell him.

517
00:40:45,317 --> 00:40:47,403
I have to do this.

518
00:40:49,655 --> 00:40:51,323
I know.

519
00:40:51,723 --> 00:40:55,223
[Edit: Body-heat warming lecture]

520
00:40:57,663 --> 00:41:00,207
Frank told me about Sam.

521
00:41:00,249 --> 00:41:02,585
I'm not gonna try
to talk you out of going,

522
00:41:02,668 --> 00:41:05,462
but there's something
I need for you to do first.

523
00:41:05,546 --> 00:41:08,257
You have to explain your results
to the administration.

524
00:41:08,340 --> 00:41:10,426
I already tried that.
I know this time
will be different.

525
00:41:10,468 --> 00:41:13,304
You're gonna brief
the president directly.

526
00:41:15,931 --> 00:41:17,892
[ Officer ]
Is that the last of it?

527
00:41:17,933 --> 00:41:20,519
[ Laura ]
Yeah. Pretty much.
Okay.

528
00:41:20,603 --> 00:41:23,105
We also found this radio,
but I don't think it works.

529
00:41:23,189 --> 00:41:25,900
Let me see it.

530
00:41:25,941 --> 00:41:29,278
[ Barking ]

531
00:41:30,988 --> 00:41:33,532
Buddha, be quiet!

532
00:41:33,616 --> 00:41:37,203
You ain't even supposed
to be in here anyway.

533
00:41:37,286 --> 00:41:42,041
- [ Barking Continues ]
- [ Loud Rumbling ]

534
00:41:47,296 --> 00:41:50,090
[ Rumbling Continues ]

535
00:42:20,704 --> 00:42:24,083
[ Barking Continues ]

536
00:42:31,840 --> 00:42:33,342
Come on, guys.

537
00:42:40,140 --> 00:42:42,351
Oh, my God.

538
00:42:51,777 --> 00:42:54,363
[ Glass Shattering ]

539
00:43:04,790 --> 00:43:07,626
The basic rule of storms
is that they continue...

540
00:43:07,668 --> 00:43:10,754
until the imbalance
that created them is corrected.

541
00:43:10,838 --> 00:43:14,508
In this case, we're talking
about a global realignment.

542
00:43:14,591 --> 00:43:17,928
The superstorm will
last seven to 10 days.

543
00:43:18,012 --> 00:43:23,183
When it's over, ice and snow
will cover the entire
Northern Hemisphere.

544
00:43:23,225 --> 00:43:26,729
The ice and snow
will reflect sunlight.

545
00:43:26,812 --> 00:43:28,939
The earth's atmosphere
will restabilize...

546
00:43:29,023 --> 00:43:33,736
but with an average
temperature close to that
of the last ice age.

547
00:43:33,777 --> 00:43:35,654
What can we do about this?

548
00:43:35,738 --> 00:43:39,199
[ Sighs ]
Head as far south as possible.

549
00:43:39,241 --> 00:43:41,452
That is not amusing,
Professor.

550
00:43:41,535 --> 00:43:44,204
Where do you suggest
they go?

551
00:43:44,246 --> 00:43:47,082
The farther south they go,
the safer they'll be.

552
00:43:47,124 --> 00:43:51,253
Texas. Parts of Florida
that aren't flooded.
Mexico would be best.

553
00:43:51,295 --> 00:43:55,424
Mexico? Maybe
you should stick to science
and leave policy to us.

554
00:43:55,466 --> 00:43:58,719
Well, we tried that approach.
You didn't want to hear
about the science...

555
00:43:58,761 --> 00:44:02,139
when it could
have made a difference.

556
00:44:02,222 --> 00:44:05,934
What exactly are you
proposing, Professor?

557
00:44:11,357 --> 00:44:16,654
Evacuate everyone
south of that line.

558
00:44:20,491 --> 00:44:24,662
What about the people
in the north?

559
00:44:26,747 --> 00:44:29,458
I'm afraid
it's too late for them.

560
00:44:29,541 --> 00:44:34,296
If they go outside,
the storm will kill them.

561
00:44:36,965 --> 00:44:39,551
At this point,

562
00:44:39,635 --> 00:44:42,805
their best chance
is to stay inside,

563
00:44:42,888 --> 00:44:46,183
try to ride it out,

564
00:44:46,266 --> 00:44:48,352
pray.

565
00:44:52,356 --> 00:44:55,067
What do you think he'll do?

566
00:44:55,109 --> 00:44:57,277
I don't know.

567
00:44:57,319 --> 00:45:00,197
Jack, thanks.

568
00:45:00,280 --> 00:45:02,199
And good luck.

569
00:45:02,282 --> 00:45:04,326
You too.

570
00:45:06,078 --> 00:45:07,955
We're all gonna need it.

571
00:45:10,833 --> 00:45:13,585
[ Wind Howling ]

572
00:45:16,004 --> 00:45:18,716
[ Generator Chugging ]

573
00:45:19,116 --> 00:45:21,516
VP vs Hall on evacuating half the country — political debate pad.

574
00:45:27,891 --> 00:45:31,520
Sorry, mates, but we're
just about out of petrol.

575
00:45:34,732 --> 00:45:38,402
Hey, is there any chance...

576
00:45:38,444 --> 00:45:42,531
that it will run... on this?

577
00:45:42,614 --> 00:45:45,409
[ Chuckles ]
Are you mad?

578
00:45:45,451 --> 00:45:47,911
That's a 12-year-old scotch.

579
00:45:55,461 --> 00:45:57,796
[ Laughs ]

580
00:46:09,683 --> 00:46:12,895
Gentlemen. To England.

581
00:46:15,272 --> 00:46:17,441
To mankind.

582
00:46:18,692 --> 00:46:20,861
To Manchester United.

583
00:46:20,944 --> 00:46:23,489
[ All Laughing ]

584
00:46:25,650 --> 00:46:29,150
[Edit: “Wish I could have seen him grow up”]

585
00:46:29,300 --> 00:46:31,700
Soft parental regret beat.

586
00:46:31,850 --> 00:46:34,886
Lucy hospital / Peter CT, Miami photo nostalgia, “wish I could have seen him grow up”

587
00:46:45,928 --> 00:46:49,389
Come on, Buddha. Come on.
Do your business.

588
00:46:49,439 --> 00:46:50,939
[Edit: Nerd clubs / radio help gag]

589
00:46:50,939 --> 00:46:52,351
Look. There's nobody around.

590
00:46:52,392 --> 00:46:55,521
[ Buddha Whimpering ]
Okay. You know what?
I'll turn around. I won't look.

591
00:46:55,562 --> 00:46:58,065
I promise.
[ Barking ]

592
00:46:58,148 --> 00:47:02,152
[ Barking Continues ]
What?

593
00:47:02,552 --> 00:47:04,952
Brian’s electronics/math/chess club flex while fixing the radio.

594
00:47:05,102 --> 00:47:07,502
Dog-at-the-door, nerd-club radio flex, leave-or-stay debate

595
00:47:08,158 --> 00:47:11,745
Hey, man.
There's people out there.
I was walking my dog.

596
00:47:11,828 --> 00:47:15,874
There's hundreds of 'em.
They're walkin' on the snow.

597
00:47:19,378 --> 00:47:21,630
[ Chattering ]

598
00:47:23,674 --> 00:47:25,592
[ Officer ]
Where are they all going?

599
00:47:25,676 --> 00:47:28,804
They're gettin'
out of the city
before it's too late.

600
00:47:31,306 --> 00:47:33,850
[ Wind Howling ]

601
00:47:34,433 --> 00:47:37,933
[Edit: Library leave debate / freeze-over walk]

602
00:47:38,083 --> 00:47:40,583
Crowd argues cell signal + walking the frozen water — group logistics.

603
00:47:40,733 --> 00:47:43,133
Dog-at-the-door, nerd-club radio flex, leave-or-stay debate

604
00:47:58,332 --> 00:47:59,576
[Edit: Jack–Lucy goodbye / Mexico embassy]

605
00:47:59,626 --> 00:48:01,587
I got it.

606
00:48:01,628 --> 00:48:05,048
You're supposed to be
on a bus heading south.

607
00:48:05,132 --> 00:48:07,926
I've been watching
your back for 20 years.

608
00:48:07,968 --> 00:48:10,387
You think I'd let you
go alone?

609
00:48:10,470 --> 00:48:13,348
All these years, I thought
I was watching your back.

610
00:48:13,432 --> 00:48:17,060
Where are the keys?
In the truck.

611
00:48:17,144 --> 00:48:19,563
Where do you think
you're going?

612
00:48:19,646 --> 00:48:22,482
Neither one of you
knows how to navigate
worth a damn.

613
00:48:22,524 --> 00:48:24,610
Without me, you'll
end up in Cleveland.

614
00:48:26,653 --> 00:48:30,073
I'll try to give you
updates on the storm
as it heads your way.

615
00:48:30,157 --> 00:48:32,826
Good luck, Jack.

616
00:48:32,910 --> 00:48:34,745
[ Engine Starts ]

617
00:48:45,964 --> 00:48:48,967
[ Woman Reporting ]
Breaking news from
the U.S.-Mexico border.

618
00:48:49,009 --> 00:48:52,429
Just half an hour ago,
Mexican officials
closed the border...

619
00:48:52,512 --> 00:48:56,391
in the light
of so many U.S. refugees
who are fleeing south...

620
00:48:56,475 --> 00:48:58,810
in the wake
of the approaching storm.

621
00:48:58,852 --> 00:49:02,397
These people came here
in anticipation of crossing
into Mexico.

622
00:49:02,481 --> 00:49:05,275
Instead, they've been met
with closed gates--

623
00:49:05,400 --> 00:49:07,361
Here it is.

624
00:49:07,411 --> 00:49:08,911
[Edit: Rio Grande reverse-immigration news]

625
00:49:08,911 --> 00:49:12,199
Oh, this fireplace
probably hasn't been used
in about a hundred years.

626
00:49:14,993 --> 00:49:17,204
All right.

627
00:49:17,604 --> 00:49:20,004
TV package on Americans wading into Mexico.

628
00:49:22,376 --> 00:49:25,879
- What are you doing?
- What do you think
we were gonna burn?

629
00:49:25,962 --> 00:49:29,508
You can't burn books.
No. Absolutely not!

630
00:49:29,549 --> 00:49:33,011
You wanna freeze to death?

631
00:49:34,221 --> 00:49:38,225
I'll go get some more.
I'll help you.

632
00:49:38,308 --> 00:49:40,227
I'm going with them.

633
00:49:41,728 --> 00:49:44,940
Okay. Do you have
a cafeteria or a lunchroom?

634
00:49:45,023 --> 00:49:47,526
Just an employees' lounge
with a few vending machines.

635
00:49:47,567 --> 00:49:50,654
[ Buddha Barking ]

636
00:49:55,325 --> 00:49:57,911
We're not gonna last very long
on M&M's and potato chips.

637
00:49:57,995 --> 00:50:01,832
What about the garbage cans?
There's always somethin'
to eat in the garbage.

638
00:50:01,873 --> 00:50:04,000
[ Buddha Whimpering ]

639
00:50:07,717 --> 00:50:11,217
[Edit: Nietzsche / tax-law book-burning banter]

640
00:50:11,883 --> 00:50:14,594
[ Gasps ]
Are you all right?

641
00:50:14,678 --> 00:50:17,723
Yeah. I'm fine. I just
cut my leg the other day.

642
00:50:17,806 --> 00:50:21,184
[ Radio Static ]

643
00:50:21,226 --> 00:50:23,729
Did you get a signal?

644
00:50:23,812 --> 00:50:26,773
Yeah. For a minute.
And?

645
00:50:30,861 --> 00:50:35,532
Man, this storm is everywhere.
It's hit the entire
Northern Hemisphere.

646
00:50:35,615 --> 00:50:39,286
Europe is buried
under 15 feet of snow.

647
00:50:39,327 --> 00:50:41,705
And they say it's gonna
get just as bad here.

648
00:50:41,788 --> 00:50:45,208
I mean, I don't think
your dad's gonna make it.

649
00:50:48,712 --> 00:50:50,547
No. He'll make it.

650
00:50:54,926 --> 00:50:56,803
He'll make it.

651
00:51:00,766 --> 00:51:02,726
[ Man On News ]
The White House has ordered...

652
00:51:02,809 --> 00:51:06,563
the following national disaster
alert for all northern states:

653
00:51:06,605 --> 00:51:09,649
Continued exposure
to the approaching storm
is deadly.

654
00:51:09,733 --> 00:51:12,027
Remain indoors and take
all measures to stay warm,

655
00:51:12,069 --> 00:51:14,529
burning any fuel sources
until the storm is over.

656
00:51:15,489 --> 00:51:17,783
Roads are impassable
across New York State...

657
00:51:17,866 --> 00:51:20,577
and as far south
as Pennsylvania.
Where are we?

658
00:51:20,619 --> 00:51:23,205
[ Man On News Continues,
Indistinct ]

659
00:51:23,246 --> 00:51:26,583
Looks like we're
just north of Philadelphia.

660
00:51:26,625 --> 00:51:29,711
[ Man On News ]
Rescue workers say
they have no idea--

661
00:51:29,753 --> 00:51:31,505
Look out!

662
00:51:34,132 --> 00:51:36,802
You guys okay?

663
00:51:36,885 --> 00:51:39,179
Yeah.
Yeah.

664
00:51:44,768 --> 00:51:48,104
- Once again, roads across
the northern states...
- Sorry, boss.

665
00:51:48,188 --> 00:51:53,276
are impassable.
Unpack the snowshoes.
We're walking from here.

666
00:51:54,653 --> 00:51:57,906
Come on, men.
Let's get up and get going.

667
00:51:57,948 --> 00:52:03,203
We've only made it to Brooklyn.
Maybe we should just turn back.

668
00:52:03,286 --> 00:52:05,872
What for? Half the city's
frozen underwater.

669
00:52:05,956 --> 00:52:08,750
There's nothing to go back to.
It's time to get up.
Keep moving.

670
00:52:08,792 --> 00:52:12,379
We should've stayed
in the library.
Come on, men.

671
00:52:12,462 --> 00:52:15,423
Okay, guys.
Let's get moving.

672
00:52:24,683 --> 00:52:26,810
Wake up.

673
00:52:44,369 --> 00:52:48,248
What are you doing?
Insulatin'.

674
00:52:48,331 --> 00:52:50,876
Newspaper's best,
but this'll do it.

675
00:52:50,959 --> 00:52:54,963
You know, you spend
some years on the streets,
and you learn how to keep warm.

676
00:52:55,005 --> 00:52:57,924
Hey.

677
00:53:01,386 --> 00:53:03,305
Thanks.

678
00:53:10,512 --> 00:53:14,012
[Edit: Favorite vacation / Greenland with Dad]

679
00:53:14,162 --> 00:53:16,562
Sam’s personal story to Laura during the wait.

680
00:53:16,712 --> 00:53:19,112
Nietzsche vs tax-law book-burning, Greenland vacation story

681
00:53:42,761 --> 00:53:46,223
[ Grunting, Screaming ]

682
00:53:58,944 --> 00:54:01,154
[ Groaning, Whimpering ]

683
00:54:07,827 --> 00:54:09,788
[ Jack ]
Frank!

684
00:54:09,871 --> 00:54:13,208
Are you all right?
I'm fine!

685
00:54:15,001 --> 00:54:17,712
Just dropped in
to do a little shopping.

686
00:54:17,754 --> 00:54:20,465
I gotta lose this sled.

687
00:54:37,440 --> 00:54:39,776
Hold on!
We'll pull you up!

688
00:54:41,945 --> 00:54:45,198
Jason! You gotta
support Frank's weight!

689
00:54:45,281 --> 00:54:47,409
Okay! I'll try!

690
00:54:50,245 --> 00:54:51,079
[ Grunting ]

691
00:54:54,124 --> 00:54:57,168
Okay! I think
I have his weight now!

692
00:54:57,252 --> 00:54:59,004
I'm coming to you!

693
00:55:05,635 --> 00:55:07,929
[ Glass Cracking ]

694
00:55:08,013 --> 00:55:09,139
[ Grunting, Whimpering ]

695
00:55:10,974 --> 00:55:13,018
The glass is breaking!

696
00:55:15,979 --> 00:55:17,981
Too much weight!

697
00:55:21,776 --> 00:55:23,319
It's not gonna hold.

698
00:55:32,871 --> 00:55:34,456
No! No!

699
00:55:34,539 --> 00:55:37,834
Frank!
I can get you out!

700
00:55:39,544 --> 00:55:41,713
[ Jack ]
Don't do it!

701
00:55:41,755 --> 00:55:44,174
- No! Frank! Don't!
- Frank, no!

702
00:55:45,717 --> 00:55:47,385
Frank!

703
00:56:29,844 --> 00:56:32,305
[ Radio:
Chatter, Indistinct ]

704
00:56:32,347 --> 00:56:34,474
Get this convoy
moving now.

705
00:56:34,935 --> 00:56:36,435
[Edit: Gutenberg Bible / rare-books faith talk]

706
00:56:36,768 --> 00:56:39,687
[ Whispering ]
I know. I know.

707
00:56:39,729 --> 00:56:42,440
Mr. President.
All right.

708
00:56:42,524 --> 00:56:45,443
I'm sorry, sir.
We can't wait any longer.

709
00:56:45,527 --> 00:56:47,862
We're the last ones.

710
00:56:47,946 --> 00:56:49,864
All right.

711
00:57:05,213 --> 00:57:07,882
[ Pilot ]
Mexicali Control,
this is Delta 2-6.

712
00:57:07,924 --> 00:57:10,844
We're carrying an urgent message
for high command.

713
00:57:10,885 --> 00:57:12,929
Request priority
on landing zone one.

714
00:57:13,012 --> 00:57:14,139
[ Chattering, Shouting ]
[ Radio Chatter Continues,
Indistinct ]

715
00:57:21,271 --> 00:57:23,314
Sir.

716
00:57:23,398 --> 00:57:25,817
Thank you, Corporal.
Carry on.

717
00:57:39,038 --> 00:57:40,915
Madam Secretary?

718
00:57:50,425 --> 00:57:52,760
Raymond.

719
00:57:52,802 --> 00:57:55,513
The president's motorcade
got caught in the storm.

720
00:58:00,185 --> 00:58:02,103
They didn't make it.

721
00:58:07,942 --> 00:58:10,236
She wouldn't
wake up this morning.

722
00:58:10,278 --> 00:58:13,615
I-I-- I mean, last night
she only had a fever.
I don't understand.

723
00:58:13,698 --> 00:58:15,617
God, she's awfully pale.

724
00:58:15,658 --> 00:58:18,620
Well, guys, look.
none of us has had anything
real to eat in days.

725
00:58:18,703 --> 00:58:21,915
[ Jeremy ] I'm telling you,
it's hypothermia.
How could it be hypothermia?

726
00:58:21,956 --> 00:58:24,167
We've all been--
Maybe it's just
the flu, you guys.

727
00:58:24,250 --> 00:58:26,586
No, no,
it's not the flu.

728
00:58:27,712 --> 00:58:29,881
Yeah?
And how do you know?

729
00:58:29,964 --> 00:58:33,343
Books can be good for
something other than burning.
Let's go over her symptoms.

730
00:58:33,426 --> 00:58:36,930
I told you. She has a fever
and a really cold sweat.

731
00:58:36,971 --> 00:58:39,057
[ Woman ]
How's her pulse?

732
00:58:39,140 --> 00:58:40,975
It's really fast.

733
00:58:41,017 --> 00:58:44,354
Did she have any injuries,
like a cut or something
that might have gotten infected?

734
00:58:44,437 --> 00:58:48,107
She was complaining
about a cut on her leg
a few days ago.

735
00:58:48,149 --> 00:58:51,152
I didn't-- I didn't
think anything of it.

736
00:58:55,657 --> 00:58:57,742
Oh, my gosh.

737
00:58:58,701 --> 00:59:00,745
That's blood poisoning.

738
00:59:00,787 --> 00:59:03,748
Um, septicemia.
She could go into septic shock.

739
00:59:04,457 --> 00:59:06,668
I've seen that before.
That can get bad.

740
00:59:06,709 --> 00:59:08,836
She needs a massive dose
of penicillin...

741
00:59:08,836 --> 00:59:11,130
or a broad-spectrum
antibiotic immediately,

742
00:59:11,130 --> 00:59:12,340
or--

743
00:59:17,345 --> 00:59:19,305
Or what?

744
00:59:39,701 --> 00:59:42,912
I've never seen
anything like it.
There's no point of reference.

745
00:59:42,996 --> 00:59:44,831
All I can see
is cloud cover.

746
00:59:44,872 --> 00:59:46,874
What are you doing?

747
00:59:46,916 --> 00:59:50,211
Taking infrared image
of thermal currents...

748
00:59:50,295 --> 00:59:52,714
sent to Houston, Korolev,

749
00:59:52,797 --> 00:59:54,757
your weather service.

750
00:59:54,841 --> 00:59:56,843
[ Parker ]
I'll help you.

751
00:59:57,885 --> 00:59:59,929
Tom?
Tom, wake up.
[ Groans ]

752
01:00:00,013 --> 01:00:02,807
I just received
some satellite images
from the space station.

753
01:00:02,890 --> 01:00:04,934
You'd better take a look.

754
01:00:16,237 --> 01:00:17,071
How big is this thing?

755
01:00:18,072 --> 01:00:20,950
The vortex is
50 miles in diameter
and growing.

756
01:00:21,034 --> 01:00:23,453
The two cells
over Europe and Asia
are even bigger.

757
01:00:23,536 --> 01:00:25,330
[ Tom ]
Good God.

758
01:00:25,413 --> 01:00:28,708
This one's gonna hit New York
inside an hour.

759
01:00:31,085 --> 01:00:33,755
[ Gasping, Panting ]
Jack--

760
01:00:44,515 --> 01:00:46,517
Jason!

761
01:00:46,601 --> 01:00:48,269
Are you all right?

762
01:00:48,353 --> 01:00:50,772
Jason!

763
01:00:54,942 --> 01:00:57,028
[ Panting, Gasping ]

764
01:00:59,781 --> 01:01:01,991
[ Barking ]

765
01:01:06,454 --> 01:01:09,916
- What are you doing?
- There's gotta be medicine
on that ship.

766
01:01:09,957 --> 01:01:12,543
I thought you said
it was too dangerous
to go outside.

767
01:01:12,627 --> 01:01:15,046
I know I did.

768
01:01:20,927 --> 01:01:22,720
Where'd you find
those chairs?

769
01:01:29,930 --> 01:01:33,430
[Edit: “I’m going with you” volunteers]

770
01:01:34,524 --> 01:01:36,651
[ Gasps ]

771
01:01:37,051 --> 01:01:39,451
Short volunteer join before the trek beat.

772
01:02:21,279 --> 01:02:23,865
There!

773
01:02:23,906 --> 01:02:26,492
[ Lock Rattling ]
Ohh! Come on!

774
01:02:26,576 --> 01:02:28,745
Damn it!

775
01:02:28,786 --> 01:02:31,247
- [ Grunts Loudly ]
- [ Growling ]

776
01:02:35,752 --> 01:02:39,505
[ Grunting Continues ]

777
01:03:00,902 --> 01:03:02,904
[ Yells ]

778
01:03:06,073 --> 01:03:08,576
[ Panting ]

779
01:03:16,334 --> 01:03:19,045
- It's all in Russian. I--
- [ Sam ] I know.

780
01:03:19,086 --> 01:03:22,673
- H-H-Hey, guys, I found it.
- What? How do you know?

781
01:03:22,757 --> 01:03:25,426
Because it says "penicillin"
on the bottom.

782
01:03:25,468 --> 01:03:27,970
[ Growling ]

783
01:03:30,598 --> 01:03:34,477
Wait. This is the mess hall.
We should find some food
while we're here.

784
01:03:34,519 --> 01:03:38,689
We don't have time.
None of us are gonna survive
without food, including Laura.

785
01:03:39,607 --> 01:03:41,776
Okay.

786
01:03:43,444 --> 01:03:45,446
[ Exhales, Clears Throat ]

787
01:03:45,488 --> 01:03:47,532
[ J.D. ]
Sam, over here!

788
01:03:47,615 --> 01:03:49,534
What?

789
01:03:49,617 --> 01:03:51,744
Bingo.
[ Laughs ]

790
01:03:51,786 --> 01:03:53,830
[ Sam Laughs ]

791
01:03:53,871 --> 01:03:55,957
[ Glass Clattering ]

792
01:03:58,751 --> 01:04:02,255
[ Inflating Loudly ]
[ Yelling ]

793
01:04:02,296 --> 01:04:04,382
[ Sam ]
Brian?

794
01:04:04,465 --> 01:04:07,134
[ Brian ]
I'm okay.
[ J.D. ] What happened?

795
01:04:07,176 --> 01:04:10,721
Okay, okay. All I did
was open up a cupboard.

796
01:04:10,805 --> 01:04:13,933
[ Quiet Snarling ]
We can use it.
Put food in it.

797
01:04:14,016 --> 01:04:15,852
[ Loud Snarl ]
[ Gasps ]

798
01:04:15,893 --> 01:04:18,980
[ Growling ]
[ Panicked Yelling ]

799
01:04:22,191 --> 01:04:25,361
- [ Continues Yelling ]
- [ Grunting ]

800
01:04:26,612 --> 01:04:29,115
Pull him in!
[ Wolf Whimpers ]

801
01:04:29,156 --> 01:04:32,201
[ Whimpering, Growling ]
You all right?

802
01:04:36,497 --> 01:04:38,374
[ J.D. Groans ]
My leg.

803
01:04:41,836 --> 01:04:43,254
Here, use this.

804
01:04:43,337 --> 01:04:46,924
[ Shuddering, Groaning ]
Oh, yeah. Here. Ohh!

805
01:04:51,721 --> 01:04:54,223
It should be
over New York by now.

806
01:04:54,307 --> 01:04:59,186
Satellite readings
are showing a temperature drop
of 10 degrees per second.

807
01:05:15,077 --> 01:05:18,706
[ Sam ]
You guys, I think
we're in the eye of it.

808
01:05:18,789 --> 01:05:21,792
We gotta get back right now.

809
01:05:23,044 --> 01:05:24,921
Look,
I'm gonna go outside...

810
01:05:24,962 --> 01:05:28,090
and I'm gonna lure the wolves
out of the room.

811
01:05:28,132 --> 01:05:31,427
- When they leave,
you lock the door.
- Good luck.

812
01:06:02,667 --> 01:06:04,293
- [ Crunch ]
- [ Growling ]

813
01:06:06,629 --> 01:06:09,215
[ Yelling ]

814
01:06:09,256 --> 01:06:12,259
Aah!
[ Gasps ]

815
01:06:12,301 --> 01:06:14,929
[ Muttering, Grunting ]

816
01:06:16,889 --> 01:06:19,141
[ Growling, Snarling ]

817
01:06:20,726 --> 01:06:23,396
Aah!
[ Panting, Gasping ]

818
01:06:29,193 --> 01:06:32,154
- [ Wolf Whimpers ]
- [ Panting ]

819
01:06:35,574 --> 01:06:38,911
[ Groans ]
Brian, hurry!

820
01:06:38,995 --> 01:06:41,914
[ Muttering ]
Come on! Hurry!

821
01:06:43,374 --> 01:06:45,543
[ Snarling, Growling ]

822
01:06:45,626 --> 01:06:48,713
[ Groans, Panting ]

823
01:06:48,796 --> 01:06:50,756
[ Growling Continues ]
[ Banging On Door ]

824
01:06:52,591 --> 01:06:54,844
Brian!
Brian, open the door!

825
01:06:59,306 --> 01:07:03,144
We have to get out of here now.
Brian, get the supplies.

826
01:07:42,057 --> 01:07:44,477
[ Grunting, Panting ]

827
01:07:53,569 --> 01:07:56,030
[ Crackling ]

828
01:08:01,243 --> 01:08:02,828
Let's go!

829
01:08:02,912 --> 01:08:04,830
Pull! Pull, Brian!

830
01:08:30,064 --> 01:08:32,858
Come on! Pull!

831
01:08:40,866 --> 01:08:43,035
Take the medicine to Laura!

832
01:08:43,077 --> 01:08:45,079
[ J.D. Groaning, Yelling ]

833
01:09:14,483 --> 01:09:18,153
We're almost there, man!
Come on! Aah!
[ Grunts ]

834
01:09:18,195 --> 01:09:21,490
Brian, close the door!

835
01:09:32,626 --> 01:09:34,962
Don't let the fire
go out!

836
01:09:35,004 --> 01:09:37,173
What's happening?

837
01:09:43,137 --> 01:09:46,515
Buddha,
come away from the door!

838
01:09:46,599 --> 01:09:48,017
Come, Buddha, come!

839
01:09:48,058 --> 01:09:51,478
More books! More books!

840
01:10:45,157 --> 01:10:48,077
How long
have I been out of it?

841
01:10:48,160 --> 01:10:50,079
Couple hours.
How do you feel?

842
01:10:51,080 --> 01:10:52,790
Okay.

843
01:10:52,873 --> 01:10:54,833
What happened?

844
01:10:54,917 --> 01:10:59,630
Well, we had to get inside
in kind of a hurry,
so I sort of pushed you in.

845
01:11:00,756 --> 01:11:04,176
I should be used to
you pushing me around.

846
01:11:04,259 --> 01:11:06,387
Good to have you back.

847
01:11:11,141 --> 01:11:13,227
What are you doing?

848
01:11:14,311 --> 01:11:16,438
The eye of the storm
has passed,

849
01:11:16,522 --> 01:11:18,440
and we're 40 miles
from Manhattan.

850
01:11:18,524 --> 01:11:20,609
Shouldn't we wait
one more day?

851
01:11:23,279 --> 01:11:26,031
Sam may not have
one more day.

852
01:12:37,186 --> 01:12:40,147
What do you think's
gonna happen to us?

853
01:12:41,106 --> 01:12:43,192
What do you mean?

854
01:12:44,193 --> 01:12:46,528
I mean us.
Civilization.

855
01:12:46,612 --> 01:12:48,572
Everybody.

856
01:12:50,699 --> 01:12:53,702
Mankind survived
the last ice age.

857
01:12:53,744 --> 01:12:56,830
We're certainly capable
of surviving this one.

858
01:12:58,665 --> 01:13:02,211
All depends on
whether or not we're able
to learn from our mistakes.

859
01:13:04,630 --> 01:13:07,883
I sure as hell would like
a chance to learn from mine.

860
01:13:09,134 --> 01:13:11,428
You did everything you could.

861
01:13:14,431 --> 01:13:16,517
I was thinking about Sam.

862
01:13:22,439 --> 01:13:25,234
Jack, you know
the chances of Sam--

863
01:13:36,995 --> 01:13:39,206
I made my son a promise.

864
01:13:40,791 --> 01:13:42,709
I'm going to keep it.

865
01:14:05,774 --> 01:14:09,319
[ Man On Radio ]
Parker, this is Houston.
Do you read?

866
01:14:09,403 --> 01:14:10,529
Roger, Mission Control.
Go ahead.

867
01:14:11,447 --> 01:14:15,325
We're getting scattered reports
that the storm is dissipating
over North America.

868
01:14:15,409 --> 01:14:17,786
Can you confirm?

869
01:14:19,037 --> 01:14:22,374
[ Parker ]
Affirmative.
It's finally clearing.

870
01:14:23,417 --> 01:14:25,377
We're over Europe right now.

871
01:14:25,460 --> 01:14:28,464
I can see landmass
for the first time in days.

872
01:16:17,531 --> 01:16:20,033
How much further is it
to the library?

873
01:16:20,075 --> 01:16:21,910
It should be...

874
01:16:25,455 --> 01:16:27,583
right here.

875
01:16:31,920 --> 01:16:34,047
I'm sorry, Jack.

876
01:17:11,209 --> 01:17:13,378
Sam?

877
01:18:21,279 --> 01:18:24,157
Who is that?

878
01:18:30,163 --> 01:18:32,582
My father.

879
01:18:35,919 --> 01:18:38,213
You made it.

880
01:18:42,426 --> 01:18:44,553
Of course I did.

881
01:19:10,245 --> 01:19:13,123
[ Crowd Shouting ]

882
01:19:23,675 --> 01:19:26,762
[ Shouting Continues ]

883
01:19:34,352 --> 01:19:36,271
Mr. President.

884
01:19:39,191 --> 01:19:43,111
I just received
a shortwave radio transmission
from Jack Hall.

885
01:19:43,153 --> 01:19:45,572
He made it to New York.

886
01:19:45,614 --> 01:19:47,699
He says
there are survivors.

887
01:19:49,367 --> 01:19:51,536
Thank you, Tom.

888
01:19:51,620 --> 01:19:53,747
That's--

889
01:19:54,623 --> 01:19:56,750
That's good news.

890
01:20:04,840 --> 01:20:08,340
[Edit: President Becker humility speech (part 1)]

891
01:20:08,490 --> 01:20:10,890
Closing VP-as-President apology / resources speech — trim.

892
01:20:13,599 --> 01:20:17,099
[Edit: confirm on picture (no overlapping theatrical dialogue)]

893
01:20:17,249 --> 01:20:19,649
No overlapping theatrical dialogue — confirm on picture.

894
01:20:19,649 --> 01:20:23,149
[Edit: Becker speech + Peter bedside (part 2)]

895
01:20:23,299 --> 01:20:25,699
Speech continues under Peter bedside reunion — end-pad trim.

896
01:20:30,534 --> 01:20:33,870
Jack!
It's good to see you!

897
01:20:33,954 --> 01:20:36,540
Come on.
Let's go get on board.

898
01:21:31,219 --> 01:21:33,597
Look at that.

899
01:21:33,638 --> 01:21:35,557
What?

900
01:21:35,599 --> 01:21:38,643
Have you ever seen the air
so clear?

